      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 56 đến 60:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. The familiar “chasing arrows” symbol — three curved triangles forming a loop — appears on almost every plastic package, giving the illusion of sustainability. Yet that symbol doesn’t guarantee recyclability at all. Manufacturers can print it freely; its true purpose is to identify the type of plastic used. A “1” or “2” often means the material is widely recyclable, while a “3” indicates PVC — rarely accepted in curbside programs. Confusion over this logo became so widespread that California recently banned its use on products that aren’t truly recyclable. The result is a system that asks individuals to interpret a cryptic code, where the burden of understanding falls on the consumer rather than the producer. Even for those who follow the rules, the results can be disheartening. Only an estimated 9 percent of all plastics ever made have been recycled. The rest have been buried, burned, or scattered across the planet. The reasons are structural: every community operates differently, with local policies determining what can and cannot be accepted. Some towns have suspended programs entirely, while others lack buyers for sorted plastic waste, leaving bales to pile up in warehouses. Even advanced optical scanners that identify plastics by shape or color often misread dark containers or thin films. What can be recycled is typically “downcycled” into lower-value materials like carpet fiber or polyester, while the remainder is dumped or incinerated — releasing greenhouse gases and toxic fumes. The scale of the problem keeps growing. Since 1960, global plastic production has increased nearly one hundredfold, with more than half of all items designed for single use. Plastics persist for centuries, fragmenting into microplastics found in rain, oceans, and even human blood. At the root of this surge is cheap oil and gas — the feedstock for most plastics — which has made them profitable even as nations pledge to burn fewer fossil fuels. The paradox is striking: plastics can make cars lighter and packaging more energy-efficient, yet their manufacture and disposal emit more greenhouse gases than the entire aviation industry. It wasn’t always this way. Early plastics were celebrated as a sustainable substitute for ivory and tortoiseshell, but by the mid-20th century, manufacturers embraced disposability as a business model. When the mounting waste drew backlash, industry leaders promoted curbside recycling — shifting responsibility from producers to the public. Decades later, the promise remains unfulfilled. As environmentalists note, recycling alone cannot solve the plastic crisis. Without systemic change — reducing production, redesigning materials, and enforcing accountability — the arrows on the bottom of our bottles will continue to point in circles. (Adapted from: https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/04/21/climate/plastics-recycling- trash-environment.html) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
