      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu 44 và 45:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. For centuries, medicine has wrestled with the mystery of healing—whether recovery stems from the treatment itself or from the patient’s belief in it. Geoff Watts opens by describing how easy it would be to invent a new “alternative therapy”: offer warmth, touch, reassurance, and an air of mystical wisdom, and patients will often feel better. Many such treatments succeed not because of their medical content but because they harness the human mind’s capacity to trigger healing. This phenomenon, known as the placebo effect, explains why patients sometimes improve even when receiving inactive or symbolic remedies. Watts notes that the placebo effect can make even fraudulent or unscientific methods appear effective. Since many illnesses naturally improve over time, any coincidental recovery reinforces a therapist’s reputation. Yet psychology also plays a real role—belief, expectation, and trust activate biological responses that conventional medicine still struggles to explain. Both mainstream and complementary practitioners unknowingly rely on this force, though alternative medicine often excels at creating the right emotional and relational conditions for it to work. Their longer sessions, physical contact, and empathetic communication can mobilize a patient’s confidence and, with it, measurable physiological changes. Scientific research into how placebos work has focused largely on pain control. Studies reveal that believing in a treatment can stimulate the release of endorphins, the body’s natural painkillers. Experiments by Fabrizio Benedetti in Turin provided direct proof: when patients were unknowingly given saline instead of morphine, their pain still eased—until another drug, naloxone, blocked the endorphins and the relief vanished. Although scientists do not yet understand how belief triggers such chemical reactions, evidence shows that visual and contextual cues matter greatly. Red pills, for example, seem to work better for pain than blue ones; branded tablets outperform identical generics. Even a doctor’s enthusiasm can determine how well a medicine appears to work. Watts concludes that warmth, empathy, and human connection are as vital to healing as the drugs themselves. The most successful practitioners—whether conventional or alternative—are those who combine scientific competence with emotional intelligence. Far from being mere trickery, the placebo effect reveals the extraordinary interplay between mind and body. Recognizing and ethically applying this power could transform the way medicine is practiced: rather than dismissing belief as illusion, doctors should see it as a natural component of recovery. In the end, the “power of nothing” is not empty at all—it is the power of expectation, trust, and the human capacity to heal itself. (Adapted from: https://mini-ielts.com/1358/reading/the-power-of-nothing) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
