      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 56 đến 60:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. In southern Florida, a heated debate surrounds the fate of Florida Bay — a vast, shallow stretch of water between the Everglades and the Florida Keys. Once known for its clear waters, lush seagrass beds, and thriving coral reefs, the bay has been suffering from ecological decline. Since the 1980s, coral cover has dropped by 40%, seagrass beds have died back, and fish populations have dwindled. The U.S. government’s $8 billion plan to restore natural freshwater flow through the Everglades aims to reverse the damage, but scientists disagree on whether this intervention will heal or further harm the ecosystem. Marine ecologist Joseph Zieman argues that a lack of freshwater is the bay’s core problem. He blames a century of Everglades drainage, which reduced freshwater inflows and increased salinity. The resulting hypersaline conditions, he says, killed seagrasses; their decay released nutrients that triggered algae blooms, turning the bay’s waters cloudy and blocking sunlight — a destructive cycle of death and turbidity. In his view, restoring freshwater flow will rebalance salinity and revive the bay’s ecology. However, Brian Lapointe of the Harbour Branch Oceanographic Institution disputes this explanation. He contends that seagrasses can tolerate current salinity levels and that previous increases in freshwater runoff during the 1990s worsened conditions. Nutrient- rich water from fertilized farmland, he says, flooded the bay with nitrogen, promoting massive plankton blooms and further seagrass die-offs. Because coral reefs thrive only in nutrient-poor environments, Lapointe warns that additional freshwater carrying nitrogen could devastate the Keys’ reef system. Bill Kruczynski of the Environmental Protection Agency adds another factor: sewage pollution from thousands of coastal cesspits may also be fueling nutrient overloads. The conflicting theories make predicting the outcome of the Everglades restoration difficult. If high salinity is the true cause, more freshwater will help; if nitrogen pollution is to blame, the plan could worsen the problem. Solutions such as building wetlands to filter farm runoff or replacing cesspits with proper sewerage systems could cost hundreds of millions of dollars. Meanwhile, the stakes are high: the Florida Keys’ $2.5 billion tourism industry — dependent on coral reefs, fishing, and clear waters — could face economic disaster if restoration efforts backfire. Ultimately, scientists caution that “returning” the bay to its pre-drainage state may be impossible, since no one knows exactly what that natural balance once was. (Adapted from: https://mini-ielts.com/1381/reading/pollution-in-the-bay) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
