      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 56 đến 60:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Hundreds of scientists around the world are launching an unprecedented conservation campaign — to save microbes. Though invisible to the naked eye, these microscopic organisms are vital to every ecosystem on Earth. “We need them in order to help conserve the pandas, the rainforests, the whales, and everything else in between,” said Jack Gilbert, a microbiologist at the University of California, San Diego. For decades, conservation has focused mainly on animals and plants. The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN), which manages the global Red List of endangered species, has recently shifted its attention to fungi and now to bacteria and other microbes. Researchers estimate there may be as many as 100 billion microbial species—making this effort perhaps the most daunting conservation challenge in history. Microbes are the foundation of Earth’s biodiversity. They live in soil, oceans, clouds, and even inside living organisms. Yet many microbial habitats are being destroyed by deforestation, urban expansion, and intensive agriculture. Soil alone may contain half of all microbial species, but as forests are cut down and farmlands spread, much of that hidden diversity is lost forever. To combat this, scientists have created the Microbial Conservation Specialist Group, which aims to identify global hot spots of microbial diversity, collect samples, and store them in protected vaults. “The way forward is to preserve the ecosystem so you can preserve the microorganisms, and the microorganisms can in turn improve the ecosystem in a positive loop,” said microbiologist Raquel Peixoto, the group’s co-chair. Microbial conservation also offers direct benefits to humans. Coral reefs, for instance, depend on microbial communities that protect corals from disease and help them obtain nutrients. As oceans warm and become more polluted, beneficial microbes decline while pathogens thrive. Dr. Peixoto’s experiments using probiotic microbes have shown early success in restoring coral health, suggesting that preserving microbial balance may help protect entire marine ecosystems. Likewise, in deserts, microbial crusts remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, helping slow climate change. “Five hundred acres of that desert will sequester a lot more carbon than one acre will,” Dr. Gilbert noted. Protecting microbes could also transform agriculture. Instead of relying on costly chemical fertilizers, farmers could cultivate soil bacteria that naturally draw nitrogen from the air, saving money and reducing environmental damage. Such examples highlight how microbial conservation supports both ecological stability and human prosperity. As Dr. Peixoto emphasized, microbes are not just passive background life forms — they are active engineers of the planet’s health. Preserving them means safeguarding the living systems that sustain us all. “The more microbes we save,” she said, “the more they can help save us.” (Adapted from: https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/17/science/microbes-endangered- preservation.html) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
