      "sectionName": "Sử dụng Tiếng Anh (300đ)",
      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 56 đến 60:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Tekedra Mawakana, the co-CEO of Waymo, has long recognized that the story of self- driving cars is filled with lofty promises but marked by difficult realities. Speaking at TechCrunch Disrupt 2025, she emphasized that scaling autonomous vehicle services is not only a matter of advanced technology, but also one of safety, public trust, and regulatory compliance. Robotaxis cannot be deployed at massive scale as long as they rely on safety drivers or remote interventions in unpredictable situations. For her, the path forward requires patience, transparency, and incremental expansion rather than marketing-driven optimism. Over the years, Waymo has faced pressure from competitors and media scrutiny as crashes, glitches, and overhyped demonstrations shaped public perception. Mawakana stressed that the tech industry must be more responsible in how it communicates progress—drawing a clear line between what is proven and what remains experimental. Flashy demos can mislead the public into believing the technology is fully ready, when in reality it performs reliably only under controlled conditions. She warned that inflated predictions without solid evidence could damage long-term trust, not just in Waymo but in the entire autonomous vehicle sector. Mawakana underscored that moving from limited pilots to large-scale deployment requires far more than sophisticated algorithms. It involves robust data collection, monitoring systems, maintenance infrastructure, and seamless human intervention when necessary. Every city presents unique traffic behaviors, weather patterns, and infrastructure challenges. A system must be resilient to heavy rain, snow, or unpredictable pedestrian actions, and able to adapt to diverse regulatory frameworks. According to Mawakana, there is no “one-size-fits-all” solution for self-driving technology, which is why deployment will always require local tailoring and cautious testing. Looking ahead, Mawakana takes a pragmatic view of autonomy. The goal is not an immediate leap to driverless vehicles everywhere, but a gradual reduction of human involvement as the technology proves itself. She revealed that Waymo is working on systems that can recover gracefully from errors or bring the vehicle to a safe stop if needed. At the same time, she called on investors, regulators, and the public to ask tougher questions: What evidence shows these vehicles are truly safer than human drivers? How many incidents occur per million miles? When will safety drivers no longer be required? For Mawakana, these are the questions that must guide the industry if autonomous mobility is to evolve from futuristic ideal to sustainable reality. (Adapted from: https://techcrunch.com) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
