      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 32 đến 34:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Some of the most creative minds in history — from Pablo Picasso and J.K. Rowling to Jean- Paul Sartre and Bob Dylan — have found inspiration not in private studios or silent offices, but in bustling cafés. These public spaces, filled with conversation, clinking cups, and background music, seem to spark imagination in ways that home or office environments rarely can. During the pandemic, as millions of people began working remotely, many discovered they missed the energy of coffee shops — even though they had been working “alone” there too. Why does sitting among strangers somehow make us think more creatively? Psychologists suggest that coffee shops strike a unique balance between focus and distraction. A landmark 2012 study published in the Journal of Consumer Research showed that a moderate level of background noise — similar to that in a café — can boost creative thinking. The subtle hum of activity slightly diverts attention from the task at hand, encouraging abstract thought and original ideas. Later studies found a similar “Goldilocks effect” known as stochastic resonance, where just the right amount of sensory noise sharpens perception and decision-making. Add to that the so-called “coffee shop effect”, where seeing others absorbed in their own work acts as silent motivation — much like exercising harder at a gym when others are pushing themselves nearby. Beyond sound and social presence, visual variety also stimulates the brain. Working in the same home office or cubicle day after day can dull creativity, while the ever-changing environment of a café — the movement of people, the shifting daylight, the smell of roasted beans — provides subtle sensory refreshment. Architectural researcher Korydon Smith argues that these “micro-stimuli” unconsciously nudge our brains to think differently. Even décor variety plays a role: unlike the predictable design of offices, cafés offer spontaneous visual surprises that keep our minds alert and flexible. Finally, the informality of cafés may help both individuals and groups think more freely. Unlike scheduled meetings or rigid office settings, conversations over coffee unfold naturally, often leading to unexpected ideas. Architects note that modern campuses and offices now borrow this coffee-shop design — open seating, natural light, casual gathering zones — to encourage collaboration and creativity. Not every public space works equally well, of course; bars, for instance, are usually too noisy for productive thought. Yet despite remote work becoming more common, researchers predict that many will keep returning to cafés. After all, the mix of mild noise, human energy, and sensory richness offers a creative boost that even the best home office can’t quite match — and yes, the coffee helps too. (Adapted from: https://www.bbc.com/worklife/article/20210114-why-youre-more-creative-in- coffee-shops) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
