      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời câu 44:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Implicit memory and explicit memory are two key types of long-term memory. Information you remember unconsciously and effortlessly—like how to ride a bike or tie your shoes—is stored as implicit memory. Meanwhile, information that you have to consciously recall and think about, such as remembering your to-do list or a historical date, is known as explicit memory. Both forms of memory play crucial roles in how we recall information and interact with our surroundings. Understanding the difference between them helps explain how the brain organizes and retrieves knowledge. Implicit memory, sometimes called unconscious or automatic memory, refers to information that people don’t intentionally try to remember. It’s also known as nondeclarative memory because it cannot easily be expressed in words. These memories are procedural—focused on how to perform actions or skills that become second nature through repetition. For example, singing a familiar song, typing on a keyboard, or driving a car all rely on implicit memory. Even after years without practice, most people can hop on a bicycle and ride effortlessly because the memory of the task remains intact. Implicit memory mainly involves the cerebellum and basal ganglia, which coordinate movement and habit formation. Although we’re not aware of these memories, they strongly influence our behaviors and knowledge of different tasks. By contrast, explicit memory—sometimes called declarative memory—involves conscious, intentional recollection. When you try to recall what you studied for a test, the name of your childhood friend, or the capital of a country, you are using explicit memory. This type of memory depends on the hippocampus and frontal lobes. Explicit memory can be divided into two categories: episodic memory, which includes personal experiences such as birthdays or vacations, and semantic memory, which stores facts, concepts, and general knowledge. Remembering your phone number, identifying a famous person, or recalling when your next appointment is are all examples of explicit recall. While the two systems differ in awareness, they often work together in daily life. Implicit memory lets you engage automatically in learned behaviors—like steering a car—while explicit memory allows you to consciously remember directions or a new route. Research shows that factors such as stress, mood, and age can affect both types: high stress may impair explicit recall but heighten emotional implicit memories, while aging tends to weaken explicit memory yet preserve implicit skills. Maintaining healthy habits like regular sleep, physical exercise, brain training, and balanced nutrition can help protect both systems. Ultimately, implicit memory lets you act without thinking, while explicit memory lets you think before acting—and together, they form the foundation of learning and daily functioning. (Adapted from: Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
