      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu 32 và 33:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. A massive bushfire north of Sydney merged from several smaller blazes on December 6, creating what firefighters now call a “mega fire.” Fueled by fierce winds and bone-dry conditions, the blaze has blanketed Australia’s largest city in thick, hazardous smoke that has turned the sky a deep orange and forced commuters to wear masks. Officials warn that it could take weeks to bring the fire under control. So far, the Gospers Mountain fire alone has scorched more than 3,000 square kilometers, making it larger than California’s biggest blaze on record — the Mendocino Complex Fire of 2018. Across the state of New South Wales, more than 21,000 square kilometers of land have burned, an area roughly equal to 94 percent of New Jersey’s size. The infernos have killed at least four people and destroyed more than 680 homes since early November. Dozens of smaller fires are still active, stretching firefighters to their limits and threatening rural towns already suffering from drought. Australia’s summer wildfire season normally peaks in January or February, but this year flames arrived months earlier. Scientists and government agencies partly blame a warming climate and a three-year drought that has left the landscape tinder-dry. Satellite maps show vast zones of extremely dry bushland across New South Wales and southern Queensland. Root-zone soil moisture in many regions has fallen to record lows, meaning that even the smallest spark — from lightning, machinery, or a campfire — can ignite catastrophic blazes that spread uncontrollably through forest and farmland alike. The smoke crisis has triggered public outrage and reignited debate over Australia’s climate policies. Farmers say the drought has devastated crops and livestock, while city dwellers have endured weeks of choking air. Environmental groups and protesters are demanding stronger national action to curb greenhouse-gas emissions and reduce fire risks. Yet amid the destruction, Australians continue to show resilience: communities are donating supplies, volunteers are supporting exhausted fire crews, and scientists are racing to model how extreme weather may shape the country’s future fire seasons. The “mega fire” around Sydney, experts warn, is not an isolated event — it may be a preview of the new normal in a warming world. (Adapted from: https://www.reuters.com/graphics/AUSTRALIA-BUSHFIRES- SIZE%20/0100B4QG2FG/) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
