      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully. Southeast Asia is at a decisive turning point in its energy future. Projected to account for 25% of global energy demand growth by 2035, the region must balance rising consumption with intensifying climate risks and security concerns. Governments and institutions such as the ASEAN Center for Energy (ACE) are setting ambitious net-zero targets—eight member states have already committed—but progress has been uneven. Renewables currently make up just 15.6% of the energy mix, falling short of the 23% target by 2025. Financing gaps, weak grid infrastructure, and fragmented policies remain obstacles. Recognizing these challenges, Malaysia’s Sarawak Energy is hosting the fourth Sustainable and Renewable Energy Forum (SAREF 4.0) in September 2025, convening regional and global stakeholders under the theme “Partnerships for Our Goals” to advance collective solutions. Clean energy is expected to meet about one-third of the projected growth in demand, with hydropower continuing to play a leading role. Malaysia aims to raise its renewable capacity to 40% by 2035, supported by Sarawak Energy’s plan to deliver 15 GW of capacity, at least 60% of which will come from renewables. The company is scaling up both hydro and solar, including a 50-MW floating solar farm at Batang Ai Hydroelectric Plant and plans for 1.5 GW of solar capacity by 2030. These projects underscore the need for diversification in building a more secure and flexible regional grid that can integrate intermittent energy sources while sustaining industrial growth. Technological innovation is central to this transformation. Sarawak Energy is deploying smart grid systems, predictive maintenance, AI-enabled monitoring, and advanced metering infrastructure (AMI) to modernize its network. Malaysia’s first large-scale battery energy storage system (BESS), launched in 2024 at Sejingkat Power Plant, is strengthening grid resilience while enabling greater integration of variable renewables like solar. Alongside these advances, Sarawak is investing in nature-based solutions such as mangrove restoration and peatland conservation to bolster carbon sequestration, and experimenting with biomass and sustainable aviation fuels derived from agricultural waste and microalgae. These efforts highlight how technology and ecological stewardship can complement one another in building energy-smart ecosystems. Regional cooperation will be vital to sustaining momentum. Sarawak’s Post COVID-19 Development Strategy 2030 positions clean energy as a driver of economic transformation, while initiatives such as the ASEAN Power Grid (APG) envision interconnected transmission across all ten member states by 2045. Interconnections like the Borneo Grid, already linking Sarawak to West Kalimantan, are early steps toward a regional clean energy market. The stakes are high: the International Energy Agency estimates that ASEAN’s transition could create 66 million jobs and add $5.3 trillion to the economy by 2050. With forums like SAREF 4.0 translating ambition into concrete partnerships, Southeast Asia has the opportunity not only to meet its own energy needs but also to establish itself as a leader in the global low-carbon transition. Adapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/sponsor/edition/sarawak-energy/powering-a-clean- future?utm_medium=cnn&utm_source=bcp&utm_campaign=sarawakenergy Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
