      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 38 đến 42:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Researchers have developed a self-healing concrete capable of automatically sealing small cracks, allowing building walls to “heal” themselves much like human skin regenerates after a cut or scrape. The innovation relies on a bacterial additive that triggers the formation of calcium carbonate, a white mineral often seen as limescale in sinks and showers. This compound, known for its durability and stability, has been associated with the self-healing properties found in ancient structures such as the Pantheon and the Colosseum. “Calcium carbonate forms naturally from calcium alone, but bacteria accelerate this transformation,” explains Alejandro Montesinos, head of the Decarbonization, Climate Change, and Circular Economy Research Group at Tec de Monterrey. The key player in this process is the bacterium Bacillus subtilis, which feeds on carbon dioxide (CO₂) and produces calcium carbonate through biomineralization. In the presence of moisture, the bacteria use nutrients such as calcium and magnesium, capturing CO₂ and combining these elements to form mineral deposits that gradually turn into rock. As a result, the bacteria effectively seal cracks in concrete as part of their life cycle. Before dying, the organisms release spores that remain dormant until conditions become favorable again—ensuring that the self-healing process can restart years later. This technique not only strengthens materials but also offers a sustainable approach to reducing emissions from the cement industry, which generates roughly 2.3 billion tons of CO₂ annually, or about 7 percent of global emissions. A major challenge is ensuring that the bacteria can survive in concrete’s highly alkaline environment. Standard strains die quickly due to its extreme pH. To solve this, researchers embed bacterial spores in a protective matrix within the concrete mix. When a crack forms, the spores are exposed, germinate, and begin producing calcium carbonate in the presence of water and nutrients. In laboratory tests, narrow cracks measuring one to two millimeters wide healed completely within 15 to 30 days. The next stage of research involves adapting the process for real-world construction under different humidity and CO₂ levels. Although promising, this self-healing concrete is intended primarily for small fissures rather than large structural fractures. “It’s not suitable for load-bearing walls or heavy- support structures,” notes Montesinos. The bacteria can function in both indoor and outdoor settings but cannot survive extreme heat above 100 °C. Still, their spores may remain viable for decades, potentially reactivating when exposed to moisture. Montesinos envisions this material as a finishing layer, similar to plaster, that could enhance durability while supporting circular-economy principles. Ultimately, this research reframes CO₂ not as waste but as a valuable resource—one that can help build structures capable of repairing themselves and reducing the environmental impact of modern construction. (Adapted from: https://tecscience.tec.mx/en/biotechnology/self-healing-concrete/) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
