      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời câu 43:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Scientists have achieved a breakthrough that could reshape the future of reproductive medicine: creating functional human eggs from skin cells. The proof-of-concept study, published in Nature Communications, involved transferring the nucleus of an ordinary skin cell into a donor egg whose nucleus had been removed. This produced 82 reprogrammed oocytes, which could then undergo fertilization in the laboratory. According to Dr. Paula Amato of Oregon Health & Science University, one of the study’s authors, the technique could eventually allow older women or cancer survivors who lost fertility to have genetically related children. The approach may even give same-sex male couples the opportunity to have offspring who share DNA with both partners. To make this possible, researchers developed a process they call “mitomeiosis”, designed to mimic natural cell division so that eggs end up with the correct number of chromosomes. Ordinary human cells contain 46 chromosomes, while sex cells such as eggs and sperm carry only 23. Ensuring this reduction was crucial, but the results were mixed: fewer than 9% of the engineered eggs reached the blastocyst stage, the point at which embryos are typically transferred during IVF. Moreover, all of the embryos were chromosomally abnormal, either with too many chromosomes or with mismatched pairs, meaning they would not be expected to develop into healthy babies. Despite these setbacks, experts view the study as an important step forward. Dr. Shoukhrat Mitalipov, senior author and director of the OHSU Center for Embryonic Cell and Gene Therapy, emphasized that natural reproduction is itself inefficient, with only a third of embryos developing to blastocyst. Other researchers, like UCLA’s Amander Clark, called the work “an important beginning” but stressed that major technical improvements are needed before it could be applied clinically. Because the method is based on somatic cell nuclear transfer, the same technique that produced Dolly the sheep in 1997, ethical and regulatory hurdles will also be high. Human cloning remains prohibited in many countries, and any procedure linked to that technology is likely to face intense scrutiny. Even so, the potential implications are profound. Millions of women worldwide suffer from primary ovarian insufficiency or infertility that cannot be treated through conventional IVF. For them, transformative new technologies will be essential if they wish to conceive. Experts such as Professor Ying Cheong of the University of Southampton argue that while clinical use may be a decade away, the study could one day revolutionize how doctors approach infertility and miscarriage. By demonstrating that DNA from ordinary skin cells can be reprogrammed into fertilizable eggs, scientists have taken a bold first step toward expanding the possibilities of family building in ways previously thought impossible. (Adapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/09/30/science/human-skin-cells-egg-infertility) Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
