      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 38 đến 45:\nQuestions: Read the passage carefully. Modern parenting advice often emphasizes the importance of helping children understand and regulate their emotions. Yet experts argue that parents’ own feelings are just as critical in shaping emotionally healthy families. In their new book Parents Have Feelings, Too, psychotherapist Hilary Jacobs Hendel and psychologist Dr. Juli Fraga stress that mothers and fathers must learn to process their internal states before they can effectively guide their children. “Emotions affect us every day and often get us into trouble, and they have huge repercussions for how we understand emotions for future generations,” Hendel explained. By acknowledging and working through personal emotions, parents create the conditions for secure attachment and resilient kids. Central to Hendel and Fraga’s approach is a framework known as the Change Triangle, adapted from the AEDP model of psychotherapy. This tool encourages parents to name, validate, and navigate six core emotions—anger, sadness, fear, disgust, joy, and excitement—rather than suppress them. According to Hendel, mental health means being able to experience the full range of these emotions with minimal defensiveness or inhibition. For example, anger often becomes destructive when avoided or denied, manifesting as aggression, anxiety, or depression. By recognizing anger as an internal process and separating it from outward action, parents can release its energy in healthier ways and model constructive emotional regulation for their children. The book also challenges myths surrounding emotions, such as the belief that they can be consciously controlled through toughness or willpower. Hendel explains that emotions are rapid survival mechanisms, originating in parts of the brain outside conscious awareness. Defenses—like overworking, shutting down, or playing video games excessively—often develop to protect individuals from painful feelings but carry long- term costs for relationships and health. Fraga points out that fathers as well as mothers experience intense emotions, including shame and “dad rage,” yet men’s struggles are rarely validated. Recognizing these patterns helps both parents become more present and responsive in family life. Ultimately, the goal of emotional education for parents is not to eliminate negative feelings but to move through them toward calm, compassion, and connection—the “four Cs” at the heart of the Change Triangle. Techniques such as practicing gratitude, savoring moments of joy, and reflecting on bodily sensations during emotional experiences can deepen resilience and open doors for genuine communication with children. As Fraga emphasizes, it is never too late: the brain’s neuroplasticity allows parents to rewire emotional habits at any stage of life. By learning to work through their own emotions, parents not only enhance their personal well-being but also raise children who are better equipped to navigate the complex emotional landscape of adulthood. Adapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/09/25/health/parents-feelings-emotional-health-book- wellness Choose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
