      "passage": "Dựa vào thông tin dưới đây để trả lời các câu từ 46 đến 52:\nQuestions 46-52: Read the passage carefully. Confidence is not something people are born with — it is a skill developed through daily choices and mindset shifts. One of the first steps is to stop comparing yourself to others. Social media can easily make you feel inadequate, but constant comparison often leads to envy and low self-esteem. Research has shown that when people measure their worth against others, their confidence decreases. The key is to focus on your own progress instead of someone else’s highlight reel. Keeping a gratitude journal and reminding yourself of your strengths can help you shift aĴention back to what truly maĴers: your own journey. Another crucial step in building confidence is surrounding yourself with people who uplift you. The company you keep can strongly influence how you feel about yourself. Spend time with supportive friends who celebrate your successes and accept your imperfections. Likewise, taking care of your physical and mental health strengthens your sense of self-worth. Regular exercise, nutritious food, adequate sleep, and mindfulness practices such as meditation all contribute to a positive self-image and emotional balance. When you treat your body with respect, you signal to yourself — and to others — that you maĴer. Self-compassion and positive self-talk also play a central role. Many people underestimate how much inner dialogue affects their confidence. Negative thoughts like “I can’t do this” or “I’m not good enough” can become self-fulfilling. By reframing them into affirmations — “I can learn this” or “I’ll try again next time” — you strengthen your mental resilience. Similarly, facing your fears instead of avoiding them helps you grow braver over time. Each small victory — whether it’s speaking in public, applying for a job, or trying something new — rewires your brain to believe in your own abilities. Finally, real confidence comes from balance. Set realistic goals instead of impossible ones, and know when to say no to things that drain your energy. Focusing on what you do well reinforces self-trust, while protecting your boundaries preserves it. Over time, confidence becomes less about perfection and more about self-acceptance. Believing in yourself doesn’t mean ignoring your flaws — it means acknowledging them without leĴing them define you. Confidence, at its core, is not arrogance or luck; it’s quiet assurance built through self-awareness, compassion, and consistent effort. (Adapted from: hĴps://www.verywellmind.com/how-to-boost-your-self-confidence-4163098)",
